Installation Instructions
*************************

General Note
============

PLEASE SEE THE SHARC MANUAL (CHAPTER 2) FOR THE MOST UP-TO-DATE 
INSTALLATION DESCRIPTION!


Basic Installation
==================

1. SHARC binaries

To install SHARC4 on a Linux system, formally you only need a Fortran90 
compatible  compiler (this release is tested against gfortran 4.4.7 and 
ifort 15.0) as well as working BLAS and LAPACK libraries. The versions 
which come with your Linux distribution should be sufficient. 

However, for smooth integration of the Python code that is part of SHARC4
(especially the PySHARC extension described below), we recommend to 
setup a conda/mamba environment including the Fortran (and C) compilers:

conda create -n sharc4.0 -c conda-forge python=3.11 numpy scipy h5py 
matplotlib pyparsing netcdf4 gfortran_linux-64 pyscf openmm numba 
sympy pyyaml pytorch pytest ase opt_einsum threadpoolctl

This environment needs to be activated before proceeding with the 
installation.

To compile SHARC, first download or clone it from GitHub.

This should create a new directory called sharc4 which contains all
the necessary subdirectories and files. 

Now go to the source directory

cd sharc4/source

and edit the Makefile by adjusting the 
USE_PYSHARC
USE_COMPILER
USE_LIBS
ANACONDA
variables. It might also be necessary to modify other parts of the Makefile,
e.g., the F90 and CC variables.
Issuing the command:

make install

will compile the source, create all the binaries, and copy them into the 
sharc4/bin/ directory of the SHARC distribution, which already contains 
all the python scripts which come with SHARC.

To set up your environment and run any command from the SHARC package, 
source the corresponding file, e.g., in Bash:

source sharcvars.sh



2. PySHARC installation

For fast, I/O-free SHARC simulations with model potentials, you can use PySHARC.
You also need to use the PySHARC code if you want to use NetCDF output files.
To install SHARC in this way, the conda environment mentioned above is mandatory.

To install PySHARC, modify sharc4/source/Makefile and sharc4/pysharc/Makefile
as needed. Then, execute 

make install

first in the pysharc/ folder and second in the source/ folder.
Note that in sharc4/source/Makefile, the USE_PYSHARC option needs to set to "true".



3. WFoverlap binaries

In order to install the wfoverlap program, which is necessary for many functionalities in SHARC, go to the respective source directory

cd sharc4/wfoverlap/source

edit the Makefile appropriately, and issue the command:

make wfoverlap_ascii.x

If you have the COLUMBUS-MOLCAS interface installed on your system, set $COLUMBUS and then 

make wfoverlap.x

to compile the full version of wfoverlap.x (only needed for SHARC dynamics with COLUMBUS and Dyson norm computations with MOLCAS).
Note that if you only compile wfoverlap_ascii.x, then make will create a symbolic link wfoverlap.x pointing to wfoverlap_ascii.x.



4. Test calculations

Now you are ready to run your first surface hopping trajectory including
arbitrary couplings!

Maybe at this stage it is a good idea to take a look at the test suite.
The easiest way to run some basic tests is to execute 

$SHARC/tests.py, 

in an empty directory, which will test the fundamental functionality of SHARC.

To test the wfoverlap installation, go to sharc/wfoverlap/source and say

make test



Linking libraries
=================

Although the time consuming part of any ab initio molecular dynamics
simulation are the necessary quantum chemical calculations and thus
any BLAS/LAPACK version should be fine to link sharc against, adjusting
the Makefile to use a vendor provided version like MKL can easily be
done by exchanging the corresponding Makefile line

LD = -lblas -llapack -lfftw3

by something like

LD = -L/path/to/your/library -lmyblas -lmylapack -lmyfftw3

where /path/to/your/library points to the directory where your
library files are located and myblas, mylapack and myfftw3 
have to be replace by the actual name of your numerical libraries. 

To link against MKL, it should be sufficient to simply set 
the USE_LIBS variable to "mkl".




